      "problem": "Find the axis of symmetry for the parabola $y=(x-6)(x+3).$",
      "worked_solution": "From the equation $y=(x-6)(x+3),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=6$ and ${x}_{2}=-3.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+(-3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$",
